# Behavioral & reasoning guidelines — always apply

Before writing, reviewing, or refactoring any code, and before any non-trivial analysis, design,
investigation, proposal, or review: read and apply
[`behavioral-guidelines/SKILL.md`](../skills/behavioral-guidelines/SKILL.md) — its "Explore the
problem" (A1–A6) and "Converge on the design" (A7–A11) sections. Implementation discipline lives in
[`coding-principles/SKILL.md`](../skills/coding-principles/SKILL.md).

This rule exists because that discipline is easy to skip when a task feels tractable without it —
the skill's own gate is "MUST USE," which under discretionary skill-loading is a request,
not a guarantee. The floor here is the guarantee that the skill gets read; the skill's own content
governs, not a summary of it here.

## Forbidden

- Skipping the skill because "this one's simple enough" on anything beyond a trivial, single-line
  change.
- Acting on a memory of a previous read instead of the skill's current text.
